              "1.Warm- up: (5’)\nGame: Whispering\n*Aims: to review the vocabulary and structure about greet and respond to greetings. \n*Procedure:\n- Divide class into 2 groups.\n-Ask 5 Ss in each group stand in a straight line. \n- T says a word or phrase to the first pupil in this line.\n- Then, the first pupil whispers what she/ he hear to the second person in line; the second one whispers what he/ she hears to the third one and so on until the last pupil in group hears the word or phrase.\n- The last pupil says the word or phrase out loud.\n- The faster group has correct word or phrase is the winner.\n- Say “Open your book page 75” and look at Review 2.\n\n2.Practice: (27’)\nActivity 1.    Read and match\n*Aims: Ss will be able to read and match pairs of target sentence patterns \n*Procedure:\n- Tell Ss about this activity. Point at sentence 1, elicit the answer and models.\n- Give Ss work in pairs to do the sentence 2,3,4. \n- Tell Ss to swap their books with their partners, then check answers together as a class. \n- Write the correct answers on the board for Ss to correct their answers.\n- Invite some pairs to stand up and read the matched exchanges aloud.\nKey:    1. b    2. d    3. a    4. c     \n\nActivity 2.    Read and complete.\n*Aims: Ss will be able to read and show the understanding of a letter by choosing the words to complete a gapped text about a familiar topic \n*Procedure:\n- Explain that they should read a letter and choose the words in the box to complete. \n- Model the first sentence and read aloud as a class.\n+ Elicit the missing word and give feedback. + Complete the sentence and get pupils to read the completed sentence in chorus.\n- Have pupils to do the task independently. \n- Get pupils to swap their books with a partner and check their answers \n- Invite one or two pupils to read the text in front of the class. \nKey:\n1. village     2. computer room  \n3. music      4. sing        5. were\n\nActivity 3.    Let’s write\n*Aims: Ss will be able to use the target language to complete a gapped letter about their school.\n*Procedure:\n- Explain that they should read the gapped letter and fill in the gaps with their own information. \n- Have pupils do the first gapped sentence together as an example. Ask them to read the first sentence and elicit the words (ex: a city, the mountains, a town, a village). \n- Give pupils time to complete the text independently. \n- Get pupils to swap their books with a partner and check their answers.\n- Invite one or two pupils to read their completed letters in front of the class.\nKey:     Pupil’s answer\nExample:\nDear Joe, \nMy school is in the city. There are twenty-two classrooms, two computer rooms, a playground and a beautiful garden My favorite subject is English. I like it because I want to become a translator in the future. I also love sports. Our last sports day was in March.\nBye!\nLinh\n\n* Game: Pass the ball\n- Divide class into 3 teams.\n- Each team has 1 sticky ball.\n- Play the music, pupils in each team take turn to pass the ball. After the music ends, 3 pupils have the ball – stand up and say the name, using the sentence model.\n\n*Consolidation\n*Aims: Ss will be able consolidate and wrap up the content of the lesson.\n*Procedure:\n- T asks ss to answer the following questions:\n  + What have you learnt from the lesson today? \n+What are the core values of the lesson?\n- T praises some Ss who are hardworking / active/ good…. as well as encourage others to try more.\n\n3. Homework. (3’)\n- Prepare Extension activities.\nDigital & AI mini-task: “Review detective”\n- T shows a short digital review with 2-3 AI-style sample answers, including at least one deliberate error.\n- T asks pupils to compare answers with learnt patterns/picture clues and correct errors.\n- Digital/AI evidence: pupil identifies and corrects at least one inaccurate answer.",
